// Practice quiz: Installing Postgres and a SQL client
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/relational-design/installing-postgres-and-a-sql-client

::o1 one server many databases::Priya's laptop runs one PostgreSQL server. She has a database for a school project and another for a side project. How many servers does she need? {
=One server; it manages both databases #Correct. A running PostgreSQL server manages many databases; a separate database per project is the usual pattern.
~Two servers, one per database #Not quite. One server can manage any number of databases, so a second server adds nothing here.
~None; each database runs by itself #Not quite. A database is data that a server manages; without a running server nothing can connect to it.
~One server per psql window #Not quite. psql is a client; many clients can connect to the same server.
}

::o1 which part is psql::In a PostgreSQL setup, what is psql? {
=A client program that connects to a database on a server and sends it your commands #Correct. psql is the terminal client; the server does the work and sends results back.
~The server program that stores the data #Not quite. The server runs in the background and stores the data; psql connects to it.
~A database that the installer creates #Not quite. A database lives inside the server; psql is a program you run to reach one.
~A file format for saving tables #Not quite. psql is an interactive program, not a file format.
}

::o1 client works server does not::On a new laptop, psql --version prints psql (PostgreSQL) 18.3, but psql -h localhost -d postgres prints Connection refused. Which part of the setup is missing? {
=A running server; the client is installed but nothing is listening on the port #Correct. psql --version checks only the client program; Connection refused means no server answered, so start the server and check again.
~The client; psql must be reinstalled #Not quite. psql printed its own version, so the client is installed and working.
~The database postgres; it must be created first #Not quite. A missing database gives database "postgres" does not exist, which needs a running server to report it.
~A password; the server refused the login #Not quite. A wrong password gives password authentication failed, which also needs a running server to report it.
}

::o2 connect to a database::A database named shop exists on your local server. Which command connects psql to it? {
=psql shop #Correct. The first non-option argument is the database name; psql -d shop does the same.
~psql #Not quite. With no name, psql tries a database named after your user account, which may not exist.
~SELECT shop; #Not quite. That is SQL, and the shell does not run SQL; you need psql first.
~\\c #Not quite. Backslash commands run inside psql after it has connected.
}

::o2 missing semicolon::Connected to shop, you type SELECT version() and press Enter. The prompt, which ended in an equals sign before, now ends in a minus sign. What happened? {
=psql is waiting for the rest of the statement because there is no semicolon yet #Correct. A statement is sent at the semicolon; type ; and press Enter to run it.
~The server crashed #Not quite. The changed prompt is psql asking for more input, not an error.
~The query ran and returned nothing #Not quite. Nothing was sent to the server yet; a result would print a table and a row count.
~You were disconnected #Not quite. A disconnected session shows an error message, not a continuation prompt.
}

::o2 server version::Which shows the version of the server you are connected to, rather than the version of the psql program? {
=SELECT version(); typed at the psql prompt #Correct. version() runs on the server and reports its version and platform.
~psql --version typed in the shell #Not quite. That reports the psql client program's version, which can differ from the server's.
~\\l typed at the psql prompt #Not quite. \\l lists databases; it does not show a version.
~\\q typed at the psql prompt #Not quite. \\q quits psql.
}

::o3 describe a table::You need the column names and types of the table orders in the database you are connected to. Which psql command shows them? {
=\\d orders #Correct. \\d with a table name describes it: columns, types, whether each may be null, and its keys and indexes.
~\\dt #Not quite. \\dt lists the tables in the database but not their columns.
~\\l #Not quite. \\l lists the databases in the server.
~\\q orders #Not quite. \\q quits psql; it takes no table name.
}

::o3 list databases::You are not sure which databases exist on the server. Which psql command lists them? {
=\\l #Correct. \\l lists every database in the server with its owner and encoding.
~\\dt #Not quite. \\dt lists the tables in the one database you are connected to.
~\\d #Not quite. \\d with no name lists the tables and other relations in the current database.
~SELECT version(); #Not quite. That reports the server version, not the databases.
}
